                  <provenance type="#i" cert="low"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_444">Pershore,
                        Worcestershire. Benedictine abbey of St Edburga (originally of St Mary the
                        Virgin, St Peter, and St Paul)</orgName><p><q>Dan Rycharde Beerley owyth
                           thys booke</q> (fol. ir). He was a monk of Pershore at the time of the
                        Suppression.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_579">Westminster,
                        Middlesex, Benedictine abbey of St Peter</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card suggests a
                        state of some confusion about the basis for an attribution. The following
                        note has been copied down (fol. iiiᵛ, s. xvi): <q>This booke was left by
                           Islip as he was visiting the Lands of his owne monastery in
                           Glocestershire, with Thomas Seabrooke, then abbot of Glocester [etc]</q>.
                        The phrase <q>what about it?</q> was later added to the index card in
                        pencil. Presumably the original reason for suggesting a Winchester
                        provenance was the fact that John Islip was Abbot of Westminster (d. 1532),
                        though the index card does not make this clear.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Robert Hare (?)</provenance>
